'''''Lathyrus cicera''''' is a species of [[Lathyrus|wild pea]] known by the common names '''red pea''',<ref>{{PLANTS|id=LACI|taxon=Lathyrus cicera|accessdate=23 January 2016}}</ref> '''red vetchling'''<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |accessdate=2014-10-17 }}</ref> and '''flatpod peavine'''. It is native to Europe, North Africa, and the Middle East, and it is known from other places as an [[introduced species]]. This is a hairless annual herb producing a slightly winged stem. The leaves are each made up of two leaflike linear leaflets {{convert|3|to|6|cm|sp=us}} long. They also bear branched, curling [[tendril]]s. The [[inflorescence]] holds a single pea flower {{convert|1|to|1.5|cm|sp=us}} wide which is a varying shade of red. The fruit is a hairless [[Dehiscence (botany)|dehiscent]] legume pod.

This is one pea species known to cause [[lathyrism]]; nevertheless, as ''cicerchia'' it figured among the comestibles enjoyed by the fortunate Milanese, listed at length by [[Bonvesin de la Riva]] in his "Marvels of Milan" (1288).<ref>Noted by John Dickie, ''Delizia! The Epic History of Italians and Their Food'' (New York, 2008), p. 37.</ref>

